[h1]Build. Deploy. Compete.[/h1]
Ranked Battles brings asynchronous fleet battles to From the Depths. Submit your designs, arrange your formation, and face fleets submitted by other players. Your opponent does not need to be online.

Battles run locally on your PC at normal game speed. The online service stores submitted fleets, finds opponents, and records event ratings and results.

[h2]Updates and server access[/h2]
[b]Please use the latest version of Ranked Battles.[/b] The server enforces a minimum supported version. Versions below that minimum cannot register, sign in, submit fleets, or start matches.

In FTD, open [b]Content → Mods → Download[/b], update Ranked Battles, then restart the game. A Steam Workshop download does not replace the copy already installed through FTD's mod menu. Finish any active match before restarting.

[h2]Features[/h2]
[list]
[*]A dedicated Ranked Battles entry in the main menu.
[*]Separate ratings, leaderboards, match history, and fleet submissions for each event.
[*]Multiple blueprints, per-blueprint quantities, and a drag-and-drop formation editor with altitude and heading controls.
[*]Named fleets that can be retrieved after signing in.
[*]Native FTD spectator cameras, vehicle tracking, and configurable spectator shortcuts.
[*]Korean, Japanese, and English mod UI, selected with flag buttons in the Account tab. The base game's language is unchanged.
[*]Campaign-based starter opponents, so you can play while the community grows.
[*]General and event chat with message/nickname search, local user blocking, and a visible send cooldown.
[*]Custom RGB nickname colors shared across chat, rankings, matches, and history.
[*]Optional verified recovery email, username lookup, and temporary-password recovery.
[/list]

[h2]Current events[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]100k Materials - Ocean[/b] — Neter; 100,000 materials; up to 10 blueprint types and 10 deployed units.
[*][b]500k Materials - Ocean[/b] — Neter; 500,000 materials; up to 5 blueprint types and 5 deployed units.
[*][b]250k Materials - Land[/b] — Ashes of the Empire; 250,000 materials; up to 5 blueprint types and 5 deployed units.
[/list]
Event names describe the budget and battlefield environment, not a vehicle class restriction. All vehicle types are allowed, including ships, submarines, aircraft, and hovers, subject to each event's budget, deployment limits, and other rules.

The team budget covers [b]vehicle spawn costs plus starting supply materials[/b]. Events and their rules are managed online; check the in-game event page for the current settings.

[h2]Getting started[/h2]
[olist]
[*]Subscribe to this item.
[*]In FTD, open [b]Content → Mods → Download[/b], select Ranked Battles, and install it. Restart the game after installing or updating the mod.
[*]Open [b]Ranked Battles[/b] from the main menu.
[*]Create an account with a username, public nickname, and password. You can optionally add a recovery email and verify it after signup. This account is separate from Steam.
[*]Choose an event, add your blueprints, set quantities and formation, then submit your fleet.
[*]Find an opponent, start the battle, and watch your fleet fight.
[/olist]

[h2]Account recovery[/h2]
Add an optional recovery email during signup, then enter the 8-digit code within 10 minutes to link it. Leaving it blank is allowed. If sending fails, your account is still created and you can retry later.

In [b]Account[/b], you can also link or change a private recovery email using your current password and an email code. Your old address stays linked until the new one is verified.

Choose [b]Find account[/b] and verify a code sent to your linked email to see your usernames. Select an account to receive a temporary password at that same address. Sign in within 24 hours and set a new password. Resetting signs out previous sessions.

Email is optional. Link and verify it before losing access; otherwise recovery requires administrator assistance. Free email delivery limits apply.

[h2]Ratings and match rules[/h2]
Opponents are selected from nearby ratings. Results affect both participants' ratings for that event, including results from matches run by an opponent.

Current battles have a [b]10-minute limit[/b]. Elimination ends a battle early. At the time limit, the team with the higher [b]surviving vehicle material value + remaining supply materials[/b] wins. Totals are compared to three decimal places; equal totals produce a draw. Vehicle value uses the surviving blocks' costs in the host's game environment, including turrets and carried vehicles. Destroyed blocks are excluded; partial damage to a surviving block does not reduce its material value. Supplies include both vehicle stores and the team's remaining reserve. Matches issued before this rule change keep their original judging rules.

Changing blueprints or quantities has an event-specific cooldown, currently [b]1 minute[/b] in all three events. Formation positions, headings, altitudes, and fleet names can be edited without that cooldown. Active matches must be resolved before changing the submitted fleet.

Quitting the game does not cancel a match. An unfinished match becomes a loss when its submission deadline expires. Downloaded opponent blueprints and temporary battle files are cleaned up after the match; your own fleet and match history are retained.

Saved results retry after connection failures or when you sign in again. Status banners show ready battles, locked fleets and pending results, with retry controls. Recovery does not count a match twice.

[h2]Chat[/h2]
The [b]Chat[/b] tab includes General and channels for events you have entered. Messages allow 300 characters, with a [b]30-second account cooldown[/b] shared across channels and reconnects. A countdown appears above the input.

Search text or nicknames in Korean, Japanese or English, 1,000 messages at a time. [b]Search older logs[/b] continues; [b]Latest[/b] returns to live chat. Click a nickname to block its messages locally.

Choose a shared nickname color in [b]Account → Nickname color[/b] using RGB values or a HEX code. Other players see it in chat, rankings, matches, and history. You can restore the default color; rating numbers retain their existing rank colors.

Chat logs are retained without automatic deletion and can be reviewed or exported by administrators. Administrators can hide messages and mute accounts; hidden messages remain in the administrator log. Service usage and storage limits may temporarily prevent new messages.

[h2]Spectator controls[/h2]
[list]
[*][b]Shift + F6[/b] — Free camera
[*][b]Shift + F7 / F8[/b] — Previous / next vehicle
[*][b]Shift + F9[/b] — Overhead view
[*][b]Esc[/b] — Ranked spectator menu
[/list]
Change or disable these shortcuts in [b]Options → Ranked Battles[/b]. Modifier combinations are supported. Native camera controls follow the game's own settings. Vehicle interaction menus and AI/strategic control windows are disabled during ranked spectating.

[h2]Beta notes[/h2]
Ranked Battles respects players' choice of mods and playstyles. Other mods are welcome, so you can experiment with your own designs and combat setups. Battles run locally on each player's PC using their installed mods, with a community ranking built on player trust.

An internet connection is required for accounts, fleet submissions, matchmaking, and results. Submitted blueprints are made available to matched opponents. Tested with FTD 4.3.4.1 on Windows; Linux and macOS have not yet been tested in-game.

Screenshots show an isolated demo session and may show older event names. The leaderboard names and ratings are sample data; the interface and combat captures are from the actual mod. The cover is AI-assisted promotional artwork.

